          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "سكين (sikkīn) is usually traced to Aramaic sakkīnā, \"knife\" (EtymDB), with Hebrew sakkin as a cousin. Lane lists it under s-k-n, \"to be still\", and quotes the old explanation that a knife stills the animal it slaughters; that is a guess of the Arabic grammarians, a folk etymology."
            }
          ]
        },
